WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.150
- We're about to watch
TLC's show, "Body Bizarre."

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:04.890
Again, don't love the title.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.300
I've said that time and time again

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.133
with many of these shows,

00:00:07.133 --> 00:00:08.520
but I think there's a lot to learn,

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.190
not only from a medical
standpoint, but from a compassion

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.230
and empathy standpoint,
let's check it out.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:14.063
Pee woo.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.230
- [Narrator] Home to
Claudio Vieira de Oliveira,

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:23.040
a 37-year-old with a curious condition.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.140
- Wow, there's some kind of contractures,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.052
likely to be congenital,
but could be traumatic.

00:00:28.052 --> 00:00:28.885
(speaking foreign language)

00:00:28.885 --> 00:00:30.570
- [Translator] No one has
ever been able to explain

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.760
the reason I was born
with this disability.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.320
- Obviously you could see
that there's deformities

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.140
throughout the body, hands,
feet, the spinal cord

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.330
there's some involvement, and
it's likely something to do

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.920
with the musculoskeletal system

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.200
'cause otherwise it looks
like he's functional.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:44.370
He's cognitively there.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:45.570
He's telling a story.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.990
- [Narrator] His spinal
column is so severely twisted

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:52.527
that his head rests facing backwards.

00:00:52.527 --> 00:00:53.940
(speaking foreign language)

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.250
- [Translator] When I was born,

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.553
the doctor said I wouldn't
live longer than 24 hours.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.590
- [Narrator] The doctor was wrong.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.240
Claudio survived.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.710
Happily and pain free, his
parents having lost faith

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:12.150
in doctor's opinions,

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.400
never brought him back to the hospital.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.540
- Wow, risky.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.600
Obviously, given the
nature of this condition,

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.390
it's possible that in the
area where this person lives

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:22.860
they might not have medical care

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.570
where they would have these answers even.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.610
They would need someone
very sub-subspecialized

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.440
for a condition that's this rare.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.450
The fact that he's not in
pain is really incredible

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:31.710
because it looks like there's a lot

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:34.680
of muscular imbalance that
would likely result of this.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:35.940
Maybe even nerve impingements

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.050
where there's pressure on the nerve

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.180
and you're gonna start
getting abnormal sensations

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.013
down the body.

00:01:40.013 --> 00:01:42.751
- [Translator] I have never
contemplated giving up.

00:01:42.751 --> 00:01:47.751
Maybe this strength comes from
my family and friend support.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.860
- Having a strong support system,

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.740
especially one from a
family, goes such a long way.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.600
Knowing that there's
people who care for you.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.150
- As a youngster, Claudio was determined

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.860
to get in education.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.690
- It's interesting looking at someone

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.880
who obviously had to
face a lot of challenges

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.220
to use equipment that is not made for him.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:08.040
It's not easily accessible
and yet he's finding a way

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:09.180
to persevere through it.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.980
So, it's important to show how adaptable

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:13.770
the human mind can be when
it is faced with a challenge.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.690
- [Narrator] The hard work paid off.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.120
Today, Claudio is an accountant.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:18.953
- Wow.

00:02:18.953 --> 00:02:21.600
- [Translator] I am someone who
managed to adapt to my body.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.010
I don't see myself as being
different from a normal person.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:29.130
- This is his perspective and
we validate this perspective.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.600
We should validate this perspective.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.560
And at the same time,

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.110
if another individual sees him
and views him as different,

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:37.440
but is still very encouraging,
is very fair to him, kind,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:38.550
that is also acceptable.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.970
We as humans need to have a wide variety

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:45.030
of emotions and a wide variety of opinions

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.010
in terms of how we
interact with the world.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:51.720
Now, a person who experiences
a congenital situation

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.460
like his may look at it and say,

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.050
I wish I could change certain things

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:57.480
about my body and get medical care

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.760
in order to improve certain
aspects of their lives.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.110
That is totally reasonable as well.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.970
- [Narrator] He has had pain in his legs

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.160
from shuffling across the hard floor.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.450
- See, that's why I found it weird

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:07.320
when in the beginning they said

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.580
that he wasn't experiencing pain.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:11.580
Just biomechanically the
way that he moves around

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:12.420
in his position,

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.910
you could see that there's
gonna be muscular strain,

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:17.130
nerve strain, ligamentous strain,

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:20.460
and a skin breakdown from
constantly putting pressure

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:21.990
on certain areas of his body.

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:25.680
- [Claudio] When I got shoes,
my life became much easier.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.080
- [Narrator] Which makes
an afternoon outing

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.520
even more comfortable.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.010
- It's great to see his
family be around him

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.410
and other children, especially,
'cause it teaches them

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.330
from a young age that
humans are different.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.890
We come in all shapes and sizes

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.560
and there is no perfectly normal human.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:41.393
We're all different.

00:03:41.393 --> 00:03:43.410
- [Narrator] Dr. Angelina Acosta,

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:46.410
a medical geneticist,
has become fascinated

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:50.100
by Claudio's case and wants
to examine him in person.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.030
- Hm.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.310
- It's really rare to see a patient

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:55.230
with all these limitations survive.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.630
- Yeah, because there's
cardiopulmonary compromise

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:00.030
when there is such severe
deformities of the spine.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.620
You need to really get a good sense

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.800
of what the anatomy is like,
the circulation in the body.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.290
Because remember, like
we have balance center

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.540
in our ears that is largely
dependent on gravity,

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:10.890
but when your head is constantly

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:13.140
in an upside down
orientation, it has to adapt.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.180
So, it's gonna be
interesting to investigate

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.580
how that's happening,
what could be improved,

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:19.050
what could be learned from this.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.510
This is only obviously
if he wants to do that.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:24.030
- [Narrator] Claudio
agrees to see the doctor.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.650
- [Translator] The thought
of surgery scares me

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:28.050
because I don't know
what the result will be.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:31.443
If I had surgery, would
I be the same Claudio?

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:33.900
Or would I be worse?

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:37.230
- That's a important question
to ask for any surgery.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:38.940
That is essentially trying to figure out,

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.370
is medical intervention, A, necessary,

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:43.290
and do the benefits outweigh the risks?

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:46.680
- [Narrator] She's eager to
know what caused his deformities

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:48.690
and sends him for x-rays.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.310
- That's not gonna answer the question,

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:52.950
more so, just show the
bones within the body.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.200
Genetic testing is probably
the way to figure that out.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:59.010
A lot of genetic conditions
don't necessarily have to occur

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:00.300
because you inherited them.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.720
Sometimes there could be
spontaneous abnormalities

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.540
in genes that can cause birth defects

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.930
or situations to even arise in adulthood.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:12.660
- [Narrator] Claudio is
surprisingly healthy.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:14.790
And as for any medical intervention,

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:17.670
Dr. Acosta believes it
wouldn't be worth it.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:21.510
- Interventions come from
A, need, and B, benefit

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:23.190
with minimizing risk.

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.890
But no intervention, zero,
unless they don't wor,

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:27.450
have no risks.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:32.450
- We can say that he has
Arthrogryposis Multiplex Congenita.

00:05:34.148 --> 00:05:37.898
(speaking foreign language)

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:54.750
- Any procedure that would
need to happen surgery-wise

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:57.270
would require multiple
surgeons to be available

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:58.170
at the same time.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.490
You're gonna need a lot of specialists

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:01.830
'cause there's a lot of parts
of the body involved here.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:05.361
Even like a cardiothoracic
surgeon would probably be wise.

00:06:05.361 --> 00:06:08.160
- It can be a neurological
problem, can be a muscle problem.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.400
So, it is really necessary
to continue the investigation

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:14.190
to try to confirm the diagnosis.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:15.510
- To confirm the diagnosis

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:17.610
is really for case report purposes,

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.140
for research, to understand it,

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.690
to be able to help others
in the future if possible.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:24.660
It's very unlikely that by
studying this continuously,

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.460
they're gonna be able to offer some kind

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:30.300
of recommendation that
he doesn't already have.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:33.540
- [Narrator] 23-year-old, Tom
Staniford, has been training

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.390
for years to be a world class cyclist.

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:37.410
- Cycling is hard.

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:38.400
I got a new bike.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.250
I haven't ridden it a lot
'cause I get tired real easy.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.740
- [Narrator] He suffers
from a mystery condition

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.050
that no one's ever been
able to figure out.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:46.890
- He's very thin.

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:48.510
- [Narrator] Tom's strange illness

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:52.290
has left his body unable to
store fat under his skin.

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:56.610
He only has 40% of an
average man's muscle tissue

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:57.870
and is partially deaf.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:06:59.880
- Again, likely to be congenital

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:01.770
because it has developed this way.

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:03.090
It didn't happen in adulthood.

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:04.680
Protein disorder is up there.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:08.400
Some kind of coding protein
could have a mutation in it.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.800
- [Narrator] Tom seemed
like a normal baby at birth

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.020
and had a happy childhood.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:14.880
- He already had a
unique facial structure.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:17.640
So, maybe he was predisposed
to this condition.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:19.470
- [Narrator] By the
time he was a teenager,

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:23.190
his body had completely
stopped metabolizing fat,

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:25.290
leading to drastic weight loss.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:29.760
He's 6'3" and weighs only 133 pounds.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:30.919
- Wow.

00:07:30.919 --> 00:07:31.752
Just for comparison's sake,

00:07:31.752 --> 00:07:34.020
I'm 6'3", 195 pounds right now.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.060
A lot of people will forget the fact

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:39.870
that fat is an important
macronutrient and we could use it

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:42.450
for energy and we needed
to make certain hormones.

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:45.090
It's the overconsumption
specifically of unhealthy fats

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:46.710
that is one of the biggest drivers

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:48.210
of heart disease and death.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:49.830
- [Narrator] Now he wants to go further

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:51.870
and compete in the Paralympics.

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:54.960
- That's gotta be hard because
if you can't metabolize

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.350
and digest fat, you have
a significant disadvantage

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:01.533
of energy storage and breakdown.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.880
- [Narrator] Because Tom's
medical condition is undiagnosed,

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:08.130
the sports governing body doesn't know

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.320
how to classify him for competition.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.020
- When we look at the
ICD-10 classification,

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:15.690
the "International
Classification of Diseases 10,"

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:16.890
we have coding for things

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:19.740
and you can do genetic
disorder unspecified.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:21.000
I'm sure there's a code for that.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:25.470
ICD-10 genetic disorder unspecified.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:28.710
Look at that, Z15.89,
genetic susceptibility

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:29.790
to other disease.

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:31.380
That would be a code that would probably

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:33.090
get him into the Special Olympics.

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:36.120
- [Alice] When I first met
Tom, it was kind of a bit odd

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:38.250
to seem him for the
first time in real life.

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:39.900
- And that's okay to say, right?

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.660
If something's unusual, it's
okay to say something's unusual

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:44.940
and be curious and kind
at the same time about it.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:47.580
Pretending that something that is unusual,

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:51.270
just to ease your own
discomfort, may not be ideal

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:53.490
for that even person,
because they wanna be honest

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:55.380
and straightforward about
what's going on with them

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:56.213
or their bodies.

00:08:56.213 --> 00:08:57.570
They may take that as a sign of

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:01.440
that you're blocking
because you're feeling shame

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:05.190
or some kind of negative
emotion to their appearance.

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:07.710
- [Tom] Because I don't store fat,

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:09.570
it remains in the bloodstream.

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:12.330
So because of this, I
have type two diabetes.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.880
- I'm struggling to understand
exactly what his condition is

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.460
because if he has an issue absorbing fat,

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:18.750
then it wouldn't enter the blood,

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:20.040
but it is entering the blood.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.470
So, he's having trouble processing the fat

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:23.790
once it's already in the blood,

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:25.620
basically driving it into the cells

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:28.950
for energy or converting it
to different cholesterols

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:30.360
and triglycerides, et cetera.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:33.180
- There's a new technology
called next generation sequencing

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:36.810
which allows scientists to
screen the whole genome.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:40.170
This is 30,000 genes and it's equivalent

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:42.630
to looking for a single spelling mistake

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:45.330
amongst a library full of 30,000 books.

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:47.850
- [Narrator] Finally, Tom has a diagnosis,

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:50.430
mandibular dysplasia with deafness

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:53.580
and progeroid features, MDP Syndrome.

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:54.660
- I mean, I would like to know more

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:56.370
about the diagnosis 'cause I mean,

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:59.550
just because we've labeled
something based on the appearance

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:02.790
and the other symptoms and
prognosis that he's experiencing,

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.160
do we know yet the gene
that is involved with this.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:07.650
This is probably one of
those cases where the patient

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:09.750
is by far a bigger
expert in their condition

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:12.240
than any doctor they would go see.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:13.590
- [Narrator] Wang, who suffers

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:16.590
from a disorder called neurofibromatosis,

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:19.500
is plagued with the largest facial tumor

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:21.090
the world has ever seen.

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:23.760
- Neurofibromatosis,
there's multiple types.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:25.740
I believe it's a genetic condition

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:27.840
where there's little tumors that can form

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:30.990
on the skin, on neurological
structures on your body.

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:33.210
Think the eyes, the
brain, the spinal cord.

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:35.280
- [Narrator] By the time he was 31,

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:39.060
Wang's facial tumor
weighed over 50 pounds.

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:39.893
- Wow.

00:10:39.893 --> 00:10:41.850
- [Narrator] The weight of
the tumor damaged his spine

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:45.180
and torso and he only
reached four feet tall.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:47.220
- That's also gotta be uncomfortable,

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:49.410
that amount of pressure constantly tugging

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:52.380
at the elasticity of your
skin, pulling the eyes,

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:54.240
maybe not being able to
close your eye fully,

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:56.520
which can cause tearing,
dryness of the eye.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:58.260
- [Narrator] The Chinese
government offered

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:02.160
to cover the expense of
major facial surgery.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:03.450
- [Translator] I had never seen a tumor

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:06.510
as big as Wang's in my
